[EncDescription_data/units/Helicopter/usa/Sikorsky_UH_19]
HRS-1

In 1948, I. I. Sikorsky began work on the S-55 helicopter, which was capable of carrying 10 infantrymen along with armaments. The famous inventor didn’t use the older design of his first light helicopters whose engines were located directly under the main rotor. Shifts in the center of gravity which resulted from cargo movement meant that such a layout was a bad choice for a heavy craft. The S-55 had its engine in the nose section, while the power-on shaft traveled from the engine to the gearbox between pilots’ seats. The cargo compartment was then situated under the main rotor axis. This design became classic for all heavy helicopters of the 1950s.
The first prototype lifted off on November 10, 1949. After a series of improvements, it was adopted for service. The US Marines Corps was one of the first to place orders for the new helicopter – designated the HRS-1 – in August of 1950. Aircraft ordered by the US Air Force and the US Army were designated H-19 (or H-19A, a variant with armored fuel tanks).
From September 1951 onward, HRS-1 helicopters and their later variants were in active service in Korea. They were used to deliver cargo to the front lines, quickly redeploy small squads in and around difficult terrain along the enemy’s home front, and to evacuate the wounded. They established a stellar reputation, and these helicopters seemed to have but one drawback – there were far too few of them.
The H-19 was in fact the first helicopter in the world capable of full-fledged army service. Thanks to the advantageous design, the aircraft had a long life cycle and has been used across the globe. These were the first helicopters to cross the Atlantic Ocean, and they even reached the South Pole. Further, they could carry machine guns and missiles, and at times they were used to detect submarines.

